WEBVTT

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.890
A chronological split fixes which
observations may train the model.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.180
It does not require this independent row
calendar model to visit training rows

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.170
chronologically.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.407
We shuffle only the eight thousand,

00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:17.190
six hundred and forty five training
positions from twenty eleven.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.050
Validation remains in
its later frozen window.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.220
There is no recurrent state or lag window
here whose internal sequence would be

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:26.730
broken.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.870
An explicit PCG64 seed generates
one permutation per epoch,

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.410
and consecutive batches take
slices without replacement.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.410
Each recorded row appears
once in a completed pass.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.930
A different seed changes optimization
order, while the same seed reproduces it.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.990
Neither operation moves a later
validation outcome into fitting.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.940
The stream creates a new permutation
only when the current one is exhausted.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.240
It then takes the next slice, bounded by
remaining rows and the example budget.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.540
This means the final batch may be
smaller than the requested size.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.020
At size thirty two,

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:08.970
eight thousand,

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:14.050
six hundred and forty five equals two
hundred and seventy full batches plus five

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:14.520
rows.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.080
Those five observations are
retained as a final batch,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.880
making two hundred and
seventy one updates per pass.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.480
The cursor moves by the actual count.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.150
Saving the cursor without the current
permutation would lose the identity of the

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:28.900
next rows.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.320
The generator state determines
the following permutation,

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:36.640
so both current order and future
random state belong in a continuation.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.297
The objective is a mean
over the actual batch.

00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:47.467
For weight gradients we multiply the
transposed batch by its residuals and

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:48.897
divide by its row count.

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.557
The bias uses the residual mean.

00:01:52.127 --> 00:01:55.997
The tail has five rows,
so its denominator is five.

00:01:56.867 --> 00:01:57.917
Dividing by thirty two

00:01:58.347 --> 00:02:02.977
would shrink that intended mean gradient
to five thirty seconds of its value.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:07.877
Dropping the tail would discard five
recorded hours each epoch instead.

00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:09.437
We do neither.

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:14.527
A small constant feature fixture checks
the expected update with independent

00:02:14.527 --> 00:02:15.937
scalar arithmetic,

00:02:16.607 --> 00:02:18.337
including the one row tail case.

00:02:19.047 --> 00:02:22.437
This detail matters most when
comparing different batch sizes:

00:02:23.107 --> 00:02:27.117
a hidden tail scaling change would
introduce a second update policy.

00:02:29.317 --> 00:02:33.927
We choose ten complete training
passes as the main work allowance.

00:02:34.457 --> 00:02:38.747
Every trial visits eighty six thousand,
four hundred and fifty examples.

00:02:39.697 --> 00:02:43.847
A one row method therefore performs
the same number of parameter updates.

00:02:44.457 --> 00:02:47.727
Batch thirty two performs two thousand,
seven hundred and ten,

00:02:48.477 --> 00:02:49.467
including tails.

00:02:50.277 --> 00:02:52.307
Full batch performs only ten.

00:02:53.037 --> 00:02:58.047
The earlier proposed ten thousand update
cap would truncate the one row trial.

00:02:58.477 --> 00:02:59.807
Before the main study,

00:02:59.957 --> 00:03:04.237
we raise that safety cap to one hundred
thousand while retaining the five minute

00:03:04.237 --> 00:03:04.787
limit.

00:03:05.277 --> 00:03:08.367
All main trials finish
at their example budget.

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:12.327
Equal update counts would give
very different data exposure.

00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:17.007
Equal wall time would mix algorithm
behavior with hardware costs.

00:03:17.477 --> 00:03:21.427
State which comparison you intend
before interpreting its winner.

00:03:23.665 --> 00:03:27.575
Keeping SGD and rate zero
point zero one fixed,

00:03:28.065 --> 00:03:32.605
mean validation absolute error is one
hundred and eighteen point zero nine for

00:03:32.605 --> 00:03:33.415
single rows,

00:03:33.825 --> 00:03:37.255
one hundred and sixteen point one
seven for batches of thirty two,

00:03:37.965 --> 00:03:41.315
and one hundred and forty six
point nine four for full batch.

00:03:41.765 --> 00:03:44.395
These are averages across
three paired seeds.

00:03:45.065 --> 00:03:47.225
Single row updates also vary more,

00:03:47.225 --> 00:03:51.755
with sample deviation six point zero
four versus zero point five six.

00:03:52.525 --> 00:03:57.475
Full batch has only ten small steps and
remains far from the training optimum.

00:03:58.105 --> 00:04:02.115
Our earlier two hundred step,
higher rate reference did reach it.

00:04:02.525 --> 00:04:06.735
Local fitting times describe this machine
and loop, not a general benchmark.

00:04:07.205 --> 00:04:11.415
The batch comparison exposes noise,
work and progress together;

00:04:11.625 --> 00:04:15.015
it does not make full batch
incapable of fitting the model.

